; A mutual-exclusion lock between `parallel` bindings, in both lowerings
; (R-C2, docs/memory-model.md MM-PAR-11).
;
; WHAT IT IS. One lock word in a MAP_SHARED page, made with
; `sysMapShared` BEFORE the `parallel` form, so the parent, every forked
; child and every thread see the same word - `stdlib/Chan.ax`'s reason,
; and one mutex serves both lowerings without the program choosing.
; `mutexLock` waits until it holds the lock, `mutexTryLock` does not
; wait, `mutexLockTimeout` waits at most a given time, and `mutexUnlock`
; lets the next holder in. What the lock protects is the program's: a
; plain word in another MAP_SHARED mapping, say, which bindings update
; only while they hold it.
;
; THE LOCK WORD NAMES ITS HOLDER. Word 0 is 0 when free, and otherwise
; the holder's pid shifted left twice with bit 0 set when someone waits
; - Drepper's futex mutex (the one `Chan.ax` uses), with the "held"
; state spelled as WHO holds it. The pid goes in with the same
; compare-and-swap that takes the lock, so there is no instant at which
; the lock is held and the holder unknown - which is what makes a dead
; holder detectable without a window. Bit 0 is in the LOW half on
; purpose: Linux's `futex` compares only the word's low 32 bits, and a
; waiter must see the bit a sibling sets.
;
; THE GUARD. Under `--threads` every binding has the same pid, so the pid
; cannot tell the holder from a sibling. Each acquisition therefore
; answers a GUARD, drawn from a counter (word 2) and published as the
; holder's (word 1) right after the lock word is taken. `mutexUnlock`
; takes it back with ONE compare-and-swap of word 1 from the guard to
; 0, and only then releases the lock word: a guard that is not the
; current holder's, a stale one (a double unlock) or an unlock of a
; free mutex fails that compare-and-swap, answers `Err` code
; `syncNotHeld`, and changes nothing. Between a new holder's lock and
; its publication word 1 holds 0, which no guard equals, so a stale
; unlock landing in that window is refused too. (Comparing against the
; COUNTER instead accepted exactly that stale unlock and released a
; lock someone else held.) Each mutex's counter starts at its page
; number times 2^24, so guards of two live mutexes differ unless one of
; them has been locked 2^24 times.
;
; The guard is a `MutexGuard`: a word struct (MM-VAL-10a) only this
; module can make or read, so safe code can't hand `mutexUnlock` an
; `Int`, a `Mutex` or any other handle as a guard (AX3004), or build
; one (AX3085). Every guard a program holds is one a lock call here
; answered, and the compare-and-swap above is what refuses the stale
; ones and the other mutex's. It is not in the runtime's handle table:
; a lock and an unlock are one compare-and-swap each when uncontended,
; and a table slot per acquisition would add two more and a limit of
; 65,536 held locks. It is not `shared` either, so a concurrent binding
; can't capture one (AX3064): a guard belongs to the binding whose lock
; call answered it. A guard made with a `cast` is the unsafe layer's
; (MM-VAL-22), and the compare-and-swap still refuses every one but the
; holder's current guard.
;
; HAPPENS-BEFORE. The acquire is a seq_cst compare-and-swap and the
; release a seq_cst compare-and-swap or store, so an unlock
; synchronizes with the lock that next takes it (MM-PAR-9, edge 4):
; everything the holder did before `mutexUnlock` is visible to the next
; holder after its `mutexLock` answers. Across processes the memory
; that edge can carry is shared mappings; across threads it is all of it.
;
; A HOLDER THAT DIES. A forked holder killed between lock and unlock -
; `MM-PAR-7`'s sweep SIGKILLs a sibling wherever it is - leaves the word
; naming a pid that no longer exists. A waiter looks every time a slice
; of its wait runs out (at most `syncProbeNanos`, 100 ms, apart):
; `kill(pid, 0)` answering ESRCH, or `waitid` saying that this
; process's own child of that pid has exited and is not yet reaped,
; while the word still names that pid, means the holder died holding
; it - `Chan.ax`'s test. The mutex is then POISONED - word 3
; set, every waiter woken - and every lock call on it answers `Err` code
; `syncOwnerDead` from then on (`mutexTryLock` answers `None`,
; `mutexOwnerDead` says why). The lock is not handed over: what it
; protected may be half-written, and only the program can say whether
; that is survivable. After a poisoning the only other safe call is
; `mutexFree`.
;
; WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVIDE, each a stated limit:
;   * No fairness: an unlock with waiters wakes them all and whoever
;     takes the word first wins. A binding can starve.
;   * No priority inheritance: a low-priority holder can be preempted by
;     a medium-priority binding while a high-priority one waits
;     (priority inversion). Nothing here raises the holder.
;   * Not reentrant: a binding that locks twice waits for itself - for
;     ever with `mutexLock`, until the time runs out with
;     `mutexLockTimeout`. So it must not be called from a signal handler
;     or anything else that can interrupt a holder.
;   * A holder that is dead but not yet REAPED - a zombie its parent has
;     not joined - still answers `kill(pid, 0)`, so it looks alive to
;     every binding but that parent, whose `waitid` sees it exit; and a
;     pid reused by an unrelated process makes a dead holder look alive
;     for good. In both cases `mutexLockTimeout` answers timed out and
;     `mutexLock` waits.
;   * A holder killed under `--threads` is the whole process: there is
;     no dead thread that holds a lock.
;
; THE HANDLE IS A `Mutex` (docs/memory-model.md MM-PAR-8), as `Chan`'s
; is a `Chan`: a word struct (MM-VAL-10a) only this module can make or
; read, whose word is a slot in the runtime's handle table naming the
; page. Every public function asks the table for the page first
; (`syncAt`), so a call on a freed mutex, and a second `mutexFree`, trap
; with status 85 instead of touching the unmapped page. It is declared
; `shared`: every operation is built for use from every binding at once,
; so a `parallel` binding may capture it (AX3064 admits it). `mutexFree`
; must still come only once no binding can reach the mutex; a free that
; races a lock call is a data race (MM-PAR-9).
;
; Words: 0 the lock, 1 the holder's guard (0 when none is published),
; 2 the guard counter, 3 poisoned, 4 the mapping length.
(import Mem)
(import Sys)
(import Err)

; ---- the handle --------------------------------------------------------
; A mutex: one word, a slot in the runtime's handle table (MM-PAR-8)
; naming the page.
(pub struct Mutex word shared
  (slot : Int))

; What a lock call answers and `mutexUnlock` takes back: one word, the
; serial number the acquisition drew (the module header's "the guard").
; Only this module makes or reads one, and it is not `shared`.
(pub struct MutexGuard word
  (serial : Int))

; The handle table's kind for a mutex (`Chan.ax`'s is 2): a channel's
; handle is not a mutex, even to code that forged one.
(:: syncKind Int)
(fn (syncKind)
  3)

; The page a live mutex names, or the 85 trap for one that was freed.
(:: syncAt (-> Mutex Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncAt mx)
  (__handle_get syncKind mx.slot))

; A handle for a fresh page, or 0 when the table has no slot left.
(:: syncName (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncName m)
  (__handle_new syncKind m))

; Retire a live mutex's handle and answer its page, or the 85 trap for a
; mutex already freed.
(:: syncRetire (-> Mutex Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncRetire mx)
  (__handle_free syncKind mx.slot))

; ---- codes -------------------------------------------------------------
; Above 255, like `sysTimedOut` (1001, the timeout every lock call here
; answers), so none can be mistaken for a wait status.
(pub :: syncOwnerDead Int)
(pub fn (syncOwnerDead)
  1004)

(pub :: syncNotHeld Int)
(pub fn (syncNotHeld)
  1005)

; How long a waiter sleeps before it looks at the holder: 100 ms.
(pub :: syncProbeNanos Int)
(pub fn (syncProbeNanos)
  100000000)

; ---- the words ---------------------------------------------------------
; The module's only raw accesses. Every public function goes through
; these and claims no more than `effect(io)` - `Chan.ax`'s shape.
(:: syncLoad (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncLoad m i)
  (__atomic_load (+ m (* 8 i))))

(:: syncStore (-> Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncStore m i v)
  {
    (__atomic_store (+ m (* 8 i)) v)
    0
  })

; Word 0: compare with `old`, set `new`, answer what it held.
(:: syncCas (-> Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncCas m old new)
  (__atomic_cas m old new))

; Word `i`: compare with `old`, set `new`, answer what it held.
(:: syncCasAt (-> Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncCasAt m i old new)
  (__atomic_cas (+ m (* 8 i)) old new))

; Word 2 bumped: the new guard.
(:: syncNextGuard (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncNextGuard m)
  (+ (__atomic_add (+ m 16) 1) 1))

; Called by the binding whose compare-and-swap just took word 0: draw a
; guard and publish it as the holder's (word 1). Until the store, word 1
; holds the 0 the last unlock left, so no unlock can succeed.
(:: syncTake (-> Int Int))
(fn (syncTake m)
  (let ((g (syncNextGuard m)))
    {
      (syncStore m 1 g)
      g
    }))

; A 32-byte scratch block for a timed wait: words 0-1 the clock's own
; buffer, 2 the nanoseconds left, 3 the last clock reading (`Chan.ax`'s
; accounting, MM-PAR-12).
(:: syncScratch (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncScratch nanos)
  (let ((b (memAlloc 32)))
    {
      (__retain b)
      (__store64 b 2 nanos)
      (__store64 b 3 (sysTimeoutMicros b))
      b
    }))

; A block of `n` bytes with a share taken, and the share given back by
; `syncScratchDone`: the release files it for reuse (MM-LIFE-2k), so a
; wait that asks for one on every slice does not grow the arena.
(:: syncBlock (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncBlock n)
  (let ((b (memAlloc n)))
    {
      (__retain b)
      b
    }))

(:: syncScratchDone (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncScratchDone b)
  {
    (__release b)
    0
  })

(:: syncTimeLeft (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncTimeLeft b)
  (__load64 b 2))

; Charge one wait of `slice` nanoseconds: the whole slice when the
; kernel measured it (`code` 1), otherwise the clock's step clamped to
; [0, slice].
(:: syncTimeSpent (-> Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (syncTimeSpent b code slice)
  (let (
    (left (__load64 b 2))
    (now (sysTimeoutMicros b))
  )
    (let ((step (syncStep code slice (* (- now (__load64 b 3)) 1000))))
      {
        (__store64 b 2 (- left step))
        (__store64 b 3 now)
        0
      })))

; The nanoseconds one wait costs: all of `slice` when the kernel timed
; it out, otherwise the clock's step `d` clamped to [0, slice].
(:: syncStep (-> Int Int Int Int))
(fn (syncStep code slice d)
  (if (== code 1)
    slice
    (if (< d 0)
      0
      (if (> d slice)
        slice
        d))))

; Lock value `v` with the waiter bit set - already, or by this call - or
; 0 when the word moved first and the caller must look again.
(:: syncMarkWaiting (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (syncMarkWaiting m v)
  (if (== (% v 2) 1)
    v
    (if (== (syncCas m v (+ v 1)) v)
      (+ v 1)
      0)))

; ---- the mutex -----------------------------------------------------------
; A fresh mutex, free. `Err` the mapping's error, or EMFILE (24) when the
; handle table has no slot left.
(pub :: mutexNew (Result Mutex Error))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (mutexNew)
  (match (sysMapShared 4096)
    ((Ok m)
      {
        (syncStore m 4 4096)
        (syncStore m 2 (* (/ m 4096) 16777216))
        (syncNamed m)
      }
    )
    ((Err e) (Err e))))

; Name a fresh page in the handle table, or unmap it and say why not.
(:: syncNamed (-> Int (Result Mutex Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (syncNamed m)
  (let ((h (syncName m)))
    (if (== h 0)
      (match (sysUnmapShared m 4096)
        ((Ok z) (Err (mkError 24 "mutexNew: every handle is in use")))
        ((Err e) (Err (mkError 24 "mutexNew: every handle is in use"))))
      (Ok (Mutex h)))))

; This binding's mark in the lock word: its pid, shifted past bit 0.
(:: syncMe Int)
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (syncMe)
  (* sysPid 4))

; Is the holder named by lock value `v` gone? Only a pid other than this
; binding's own can be (a thread's siblings share its pid, and are alive
; while it runs), and only if the word STILL names it once the kernel
; says so - the holder may have unlocked and exited in between. ESRCH
; from `kill(pid, 0)` says so; so does `waitid`, for a child of THIS
; process that has exited and not been reaped - a zombie, which `kill`
; still answers for.
(:: syncHolderDead (-> Int Int Int Bool))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (syncHolderDead m v me)
  (let ((owner (/ v 4)))
    (if (|| (== owner 0) (== (* owner 4) me))
      false
      (match (sysKill owner 0)
        ((Ok r)
          (if (syncChildEnded owner)
            (== (/ (syncLoad m 0) 4) owner)
            false)
        )
        ((Err e)
          (if (== (errCode e) 3)
            (== (/ (syncLoad m 0) 4) owner)
            false)
        )))))

; Whether `pid` is this process's own child and has exited, unreaped:
; `Chan.ax`'s `chanChildEnded`. `waitid` with WNOWAIT does not reap it,
; so the join that follows still owns the child.
(:: syncChildEnded (-> Int Bool))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (syncChildEnded pid)
  (let ((buf (syncBlock sysChildPollBytes)))
    (let ((ended (syncYes (sysChildExited pid buf))))
      {
        (syncScratchDone buf)
        ended
      })))

; `Ok True`, and nothing else, is yes: a look that failed has seen nothing.
(:: syncYes (-> (Result Bool Error) Bool))
(fn (syncYes r)
  (match r
    ((Ok b) b)
    ((Err e) false)))

; Mark the mutex poisoned and wake every waiter, so each of them sees it.
(:: syncPoison (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (syncPoison m)
  {
    (syncStore m 3 1)
    (sysWakeWord m)
    0
  })

; Whether a timed wait has no time left; an untimed one never runs out
; (and has no scratch block to ask).
(:: syncOutOfTime (-> Int Int Bool))
(fn (syncOutOfTime b timed)
  (if (== timed 1)
    (<= (syncTimeLeft b) 0)
    false))

; How long the next sleep may be: the time left, or the probe interval
; if that is shorter - the holder is looked at every time one runs out.
(:: syncSlice (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (syncSlice b timed)
  (if (== timed 1)
    (if (< (syncTimeLeft b) syncProbeNanos)
      (syncTimeLeft b)
      syncProbeNanos)
    syncProbeNanos))

; The one waiting loop, behind `mutexLock` (forever: `timed` 0) and
; `mutexLockTimeout` (`timed` 1, at most the nanoseconds `b` holds).
; Answers the guard, or -1 timed out, or -2 the holder is dead.
(:: syncAcquire (-> Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (syncAcquire m b timed)
  (let (
    (me syncMe)
    (mut state 0)
    (mut guard 0)
  )
    {
      (while (== state 0)
        (if (== (syncLoad m 3) 1)
          (set state 2)
          (let ((v (syncLoad m 0)))
            (if (== v 0)
              ; Taken after a wait: marked contended, because another
              ; waiter may still sleep on the word.
              (if (== (syncCas m 0 (+ me 1)) 0)
                {
                  (set guard (syncTake m))
                  (set state 1)
                }
                0)
              (if (syncOutOfTime b timed)
                (set state 3)
                (let ((w (syncMarkWaiting m v)))
                  (if (== w 0)
                    0
                    (let ((slice (syncSlice b timed)))
                      (let ((code (sysWaitWordTimeout m w slice)))
                        {
                          (if (== timed 1)
                            (syncTimeSpent b code slice)
                            0)
                          (if (&& (== code 1) (syncHolderDead m w me))
                            {
                              (syncPoison m)
                              (set state 2)
                            }
                            0)
                        })))))))))
      (if (== state 1)
        guard
        (if (== state 2)
          (- 0 2)
          (- 0 1)))
    }))

; Wait until this binding holds the lock. `Ok` the guard `mutexUnlock`
; wants back; `Err` code `syncOwnerDead` if the holder is found dead
; while this waits, or the mutex was poisoned before.
(pub :: mutexLock (-> Mutex (Result MutexGuard Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (mutexLock mx)
  (let ((m (syncAt mx)))
    (if (== (syncLoad m 3) 1)
      (Err (mkError syncOwnerDead "mutexLock: the holder died holding it"))
      (let ((me syncMe))
        (if (== (syncCas m 0 me) 0)
          (Ok (MutexGuard (syncTake m)))
          (let ((g (syncAcquire m 0 0)))
            (if (> g 0)
              (Ok (MutexGuard g))
              (Err (mkError syncOwnerDead "mutexLock: the holder died holding it")))))))))

; Take the lock if it is free right now: `Some` the guard, `None` if it
; is held - or poisoned, which `mutexOwnerDead` tells apart.
(pub :: mutexTryLock (-> Mutex (Option MutexGuard)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (mutexTryLock mx)
  (let ((m (syncAt mx)))
    (if (== (syncLoad m 3) 1)
      None
      (let ((me syncMe))
        (if (== (syncCas m 0 me) 0)
          (Some (MutexGuard (syncTake m)))
          None)))))

; `mutexLock`, waiting at most `nanos` nanoseconds. `Ok` the guard;
; `Err` code `sysTimedOut` when the time ran out with the lock still
; held by a holder that looks alive; `Err` code `syncOwnerDead` when the
; holder was found dead. A wait nobody ends is never shorter than
; `nanos` on a monotonic clock (MM-PAR-12 states Darwin's bound). A
; non-positive `nanos` is `mutexTryLock` with a reason.
(pub :: mutexLockTimeout (-> Mutex Int (Result MutexGuard Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (mutexLockTimeout mx nanos)
  (let ((m (syncAt mx)))
    (if (== (syncLoad m 3) 1)
      (Err (mkError syncOwnerDead "mutexLockTimeout: the holder died holding it"))
      (let ((me syncMe))
        (if (== (syncCas m 0 me) 0)
          (Ok (MutexGuard (syncTake m)))
          (let ((b (syncScratch nanos)))
            (let ((g (syncAcquire m b 1)))
              {
                (syncScratchDone b)
                (if (> g 0)
                  (Ok (MutexGuard g))
                  (if (== g (- 0 2))
                    (Err (mkError syncOwnerDead "mutexLockTimeout: the holder died holding it"))
                    (Err (mkError sysTimedOut "mutexLockTimeout: timed out"))))
              })))))))

; Let the next holder in. `mg` must be the guard this binding's lock
; call answered: any other - on a free mutex, a stale one, a sibling's,
; another mutex's - is `Err` code `syncNotHeld` and leaves the lock as
; it was. Exactly one unlock per acquisition succeeds, however the calls
; interleave.
(pub :: mutexUnlock (-> Mutex MutexGuard (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (mutexUnlock mx mg)
  (let (
    (m (syncAt mx))
    (guard mg.serial)
  )
    (if (|| (<= guard 0) (!= (syncCasAt m 1 guard 0) guard))
      (Err (mkError syncNotHeld "mutexUnlock: this binding does not hold the mutex"))
      (let ((v (syncLoad m 0)))
        (if (&& (== (% v 2) 0) (== (syncCas m v 0) v))
          (Ok 0)
          {
            (syncStore m 0 0)
            (sysWakeWord m)
            (Ok 0)
          })))))

; Whether a holder was found dead holding this mutex (the poisoning in
; the header).
(pub :: mutexOwnerDead (-> Mutex Bool))
(pub fn (mutexOwnerDead mx)
  (== (syncLoad (syncAt mx) 3) 1))

; Unmap the mutex. Only once no binding can still reach it - after the
; `parallel` form that used it. Takes no lock, so it is also the one
; safe call on a poisoned mutex. The handle is retired before the page
; is unmapped, so every call made after this one - a second `mutexFree`
; included - traps with status 85.
(pub :: mutexFree (-> Mutex (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (mutexFree mx)
  (let ((m (syncRetire mx)))
    (sysUnmapShared m (syncLoad m 4))))
